Mii Logical Architecture: A Categorical Framework for Conscious–Subconscious Coupling

Author: Ouadi Maakoul


Abstract

We construct a mathematically rigorous model of cognitive dynamics from three categories: CR (conscious reasoning), SP (subconscious pattern processing), and Mii (meta‑cognitive coupling). Five functors define two fundamental adjunctions F \dashv G and P \dashv Q that capture the bidirectional translation between logical and associative representations. Their composition yields an endofunctor T on the product category \mathbf{CR}\times\mathbf{SP}. Iterating T from the initial state converges to a least fixed point – the state of stable understanding where conscious and subconscious fully explain each other. We further prove that the assignment of meta‑states to local problems forms a sheaf on a Grothendieck site, ensuring global coherence, and that the learning dynamics (gradient descent and evolutionary tournament) converge almost surely.

---

1. Mathematical Preliminaries

We recall the standard definitions; the reader is referred to Mac Lane and Borceux for details. Let \mathbf{Set} be the category of sets and \mathbf{Poset} the category of partially ordered sets.

Definition 1.1 (Category). A category \mathcal{C} consists of a class of objects \operatorname{Ob}(\mathcal{C}); for each pair A,B\in\operatorname{Ob}(\mathcal{C}) a set \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,B) of morphisms; an associative composition \circ; and for each object A an identity morphism \mathrm{id}_A satisfying the unit laws.

Definition 1.2 (Functor). A functor F:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{D} assigns to each object A an object F A and to each morphism f:A\to B a morphism F f : F A \to F B, preserving composition and identities.

Definition 1.3 (Natural Transformation). For functors F,G:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{D}, a natural transformation \eta:F\Rightarrow G is a family of morphisms \{\eta_A: F A \to G A\}_{A\in\operatorname{Ob}(\mathcal{C})} such that for every f:A\to B one has G f \circ \eta_A = \eta_B \circ F f.

Definition 1.4 (Adjunction). An adjunction F \dashv G with F:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{D} and G:\mathcal{D}\to\mathcal{C} is given by natural transformations \eta:\mathrm{Id}_\mathcal{C}\Rightarrow G F (unit) and \varepsilon: F G \Rightarrow \mathrm{Id}_\mathcal{D} (counit) satisfying the triangle identities

\varepsilon_{F A}\circ F\eta_A = \mathrm{id}_{F A}, \qquad G\varepsilon_B \circ \eta_{G B} = \mathrm{id}_{G B}.

Definition 1.5 (Complete Partial Order – cpo). A poset (D,\sqsubseteq) with a least element \bot is a cpo if every directed subset has a supremum.

Definition 1.6 (Scott‑continuity). A function f:D\to E between cpos is Scott‑continuous if it preserves directed suprema.

Theorem 1.7 (Kleene Fixed‑Point Theorem). If f:D\to D is Scott‑continuous on a cpo with bottom \bot, then f has a least fixed point given by \operatorname{lfp}(f) = \bigsqcup_{n\ge 0} f^n(\bot).

Definition 1.8 (Grothendieck Topology and Sheaf). A coverage on a category \mathcal{X} assigns to each object U a collection of covering families \{U_i\to U\}. A presheaf F:\mathcal{X}^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathbf{Set} is a sheaf if for every covering family the diagram

F(U) \to \prod_i F(U_i) \rightrightarrows \prod_{i,j} F(U_i\times_U U_j)

is an equaliser in \mathbf{Set}.

