2. The Three Cognitive Categories

We define the categories that capture the three layers of the architecture.

2.1 The Conscious Layer \mathbf{CR}

Definition 2.1. An object C\in\mathbf{CR} is a tuple

C = (P,\mathrm{Ctx},\tau,\mathcal{W}),

where

· P\subseteq\mathrm{Prop} is a finite set of propositions,
· \mathrm{Ctx}=(\mathrm{World},\mathrm{Goal},\mathrm{Constraint}) is a context triple,
· \tau\in\mathrm{Trace}^* is a finite reasoning trace,
· \mathcal{W}:P\to[0,1] is an attention‑weight distribution satisfying \sum_{p\in P}\mathcal{W}(p)=1.

Definition 2.2. A morphism f:C_1\to C_2 is a tuple

f = (A,B,\mathrm{just}(f),\mathrm{conf}(f)),

where A\subseteq P_1, B\subseteq P_2, \mathrm{just}(f) is a justification (a formal proof or equivalent), and \mathrm{conf}(f)\in[0,1] is the confidence value. Composition g\circ f for f:C_1\to C_2, g:C_2\to C_3 is defined by

g\circ f = \bigl(A,C,\; \mathrm{just}(g)\wedge\mathrm{just}(f),\; \min(\mathrm{conf}(f),\mathrm{conf}(g))\cdot \mathrm{coh}(f,g)\bigr),

where \mathrm{coh}(f,g)\in[0,1] measures the coherence of the justifications. The identity on C is \mathrm{id}_C = (P,P,\text{empty justification},1.0). Associativity and the unit laws hold by construction; therefore \mathbf{CR} is a category.

2.2 The Subconscious Layer \mathbf{SP}

Definition 2.3. An object S\in\mathbf{SP} is a tuple

S = (E,\mathcal{A},\mathcal{H},\mathcal{B}),

where E\in\mathbb{R}^d is an embedding vector, \mathcal{A} is a weighted association graph with vertex set V_\mathcal{A} and symmetric weight function w:V_\mathcal{A}\times V_\mathcal{A}\to[0,1], \mathcal{H} is a heuristic database, and \mathcal{B}\in\mathbb{R}^k is a priming vector.

Definition 2.4. A morphism f:S_1\to S_2 is a tuple (X,Y,\mathcal{T}_f,\mathrm{sim}_f) where \mathcal{T}_f:V_{\mathcal{A}_1}\to V_{\mathcal{A}_2} maps vertices, and \mathrm{sim}_f\in[0,1] is the similarity strength. Composition uses the product t‑norm:

g\circ f = (X,Z,\; \mathcal{T}_g\circ\mathcal{T}_f,\; \mathrm{sim}_f\cdot\mathrm{sim}_g\cdot\mathrm{compat}(f,g)),

with \mathrm{compat}(f,g)\in[0,1] a compatibility factor. This makes \mathbf{SP} a category enriched over the monoidal category ([0,1],\times,1).

2.3 The Meta‑cognitive Layer \mathbf{Mii}

Definition 2.5. An object M\in\mathbf{Mii} is a quintuple

M = (C,S,\mathcal{T},\alpha,\beta,\gamma),

where C\in\mathbf{CR}, S\in\mathbf{SP}, \mathcal{T} is a bidirectional translation table consisting of triples (c,s,w) with c\in P_C, s\in V_{\mathcal{A}_S}, and coupling weight w\in[0,1]; and \alpha,\beta,\gamma\ge 0 with \alpha+\beta+\gamma=1 are the balance coefficients (conscious, subconscious, meta).

Definition 2.6. A morphism \varphi:M_1\to M_2 is a pair

\varphi = (M_1,M_2,\mathrm{Adj},\mathrm{insight}),

where \mathrm{Adj}:(\alpha_1,\beta_1,\gamma_1)\to(\alpha_2,\beta_2,\gamma_2) is an adjustment of the balance coefficients and \mathrm{insight}\in[0,1]. Composition combines the adjustments and takes the product of insight values; identities exist. Hence \mathbf{Mii} is a category.

