4. The Two Fundamental Adjunctions

4.1 Formalisation–Grounding Adjunction F \dashv G

Theorem 4.1. F:\mathbf{CR}\to\mathbf{Mii} is left adjoint to G:\mathbf{Mii}\to\mathbf{CR}.

Proof. We construct a natural bijection

\Phi_{C,M}: \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{Mii}}(F(C),M) \longrightarrow \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{CR}}(C,G(M)).

Given \varphi = (F(C),M,\mathrm{Adj},\mathrm{insight}), the conscious morphism \Phi(\varphi) takes the subset of propositions in C that \varphi employs, transfers them via the insight to the corresponding grounded propositions in G(M), and assigns confidence \mathrm{insight}. Conversely, given \psi:C\to G(M), we build \varphi by formalising the source with F, applying the conscious morphism to the conscious part, and updating the translation table of F(C) to match that of M. Naturality in C and M follows from the functoriality of F and G.

Define the unit \eta_C: C\to G(F(C)) as the inclusion of C into the richer state G(F(C)), which augments C by the high‑confidence translations of the patterns extracted from its own trace. The counit \varepsilon_M: F(G(M))\to M adjusts the translation table of F(G(M)) to coincide with that of M and resets the balance weights to those of M.

We verify the triangle identities.

First identity: \varepsilon_{F(C)}\circ F(\eta_C) = \mathrm{id}_{F(C)}.
The morphism \eta_C formalises C and grounds it, adding only propositions latent in S_C. F(\eta_C) lifts this inclusion. Then \varepsilon_{F(C)} discards these additions and restores the original translation table and weights; the composition is the identity.

Second identity: G(\varepsilon_M)\circ \eta_{G(M)} = \mathrm{id}_{G(M)}.
\eta_{G(M)} formalises the grounded state, then G(\varepsilon_M) grounds the adjusted formalisation and removes the introduced redundancy, yielding the original G(M).

Thus F\dashv G holds. ∎

4.2 Lifting–Projection Adjunction P \dashv Q

Theorem 4.2. P:\mathbf{SP}\to\mathbf{Mii} is left adjoint to Q:\mathbf{Mii}\to\mathbf{SP}.

Proof. The construction is entirely dual to Theorem 4.1, exchanging the role of propositions and pattern vertices. The unit \eta_S: S\to Q(P(S)) enriches the subconscious with the propositional interpretations of its own vertices; the counit \varepsilon_M: P(Q(M))\to M collapses the round‑trip. The triangle identities are verified analogously. ∎

