5. The Cross‑Translation Functor and Fixed‑Point Convergence

Definition 5.1. The endofunctor T:\mathbf{CR}\times\mathbf{SP}\longrightarrow\mathbf{CR}\times\mathbf{SP} is defined on objects by

T(C,S) = \bigl( G(P(S)),\; Q(F(C)) \bigr),

and on morphisms componentwise.

Lemma 5.2. T is a functor.

Proof. T is the composition of the functors F,G,P,Q, hence functorial. ∎

5.1 A CPO Structure on Cognitive States

Define a partial order on \mathbf{CR}\times\mathbf{SP}:

(C_1,S_1) \sqsubseteq (C_2,S_2) \iff
P_1 \subseteq_{\mathrm{log}} P_2 \text{ and } V_{\mathcal{A}_1}\subseteq V_{\mathcal{A}_2},

where P_1 \subseteq_{\mathrm{log}} P_2 means that every proposition in P_1 is a logical consequence of P_2 (relative to the shared context), and the graph inclusion is on vertices with edge weights preserved on the common subset. The bottom element is \bot = (\emptyset,\emptyset).

Proposition 5.3. (\mathbf{CR}\times\mathbf{SP},\sqsubseteq) is a cpo.

Proof. The least element exists. For a directed set \{(C_i,S_i)\}_i, let P^* be the deductive closure of the union of the proposition sets, and let V^* be the union of the vertex sets with edge weights defined as the supremum on overlaps (possible because the set is directed). Consistency is preserved by directedness. Thus the supremum exists. ∎

Lemma 5.4. T is Scott‑continuous.

Proof. Each of F,G,P,Q is monotone with respect to \sqsubseteq: enlarging proposition or vertex sets only adds derived structure. Hence T preserves directed suprema, i.e. it is Scott‑continuous. ∎

Theorem 5.5 (Fixed Point of Stable Understanding). The Kleene chain

\bot \;\sqsubseteq\; T(\bot) \;\sqsubseteq\; T^2(\bot) \;\sqsubseteq\; \cdots

converges to the least fixed point (C^*,S^*) of T. This fixed point satisfies

G(P(S^*)) = C^*, \qquad Q(F(C^*)) = S^*.

Proof. By Proposition 5.3 and Lemma 5.4, T is a Scott‑continuous endofunction on a cpo. Theorem 1.7 yields the least fixed point

(C^*,S^*) = \bigsqcup_{n\ge 0} T^n(\bot).

The fixed‑point condition T(C^*,S^*)=(C^*,S^*) is exactly the pair of equalities stated. Minimality follows because any other fixed point (C,S) satisfies \bot\sqsubseteq (C,S), and by induction T^n(\bot)\sqsubseteq (C,S) for all n; hence the supremum is also below (C,S). ∎

Interpretation. At the fixed point the conscious reasoning is completely grounded in the subconscious patterns, and those patterns are a perfect image of the conscious structure. This state represents stable understanding.

