6. The Sheaf of Meta‑states

Let \mathcal{X} be the category of open sub‑problems (modelled as a poset of task contexts under inclusion) equipped with a Grothendieck topology where covering families are jointly surjective collections of sub‑problems.

Definition 6.1. Define a presheaf \mathfrak{M}:\mathcal{X}^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathbf{Set} by

\mathfrak{M}(U) = \{\, M_U\in\mathbf{Mii} \mid M_U \text{ is a valid meta‑state for problem } U \,\},

with restriction maps given by projecting the components onto the sub‑problem U'\subseteq U.

Theorem 6.2. \mathfrak{M} is a sheaf.

Proof. We verify the two sheaf conditions.

Locality (separatedness): Let s,t\in\mathfrak{M}(U) agree on every member of a cover \{U_i\to U\}. Then their proposition sets, vertex sets, and translation tables coincide on each U_i. Since the covering is jointly surjective, the components are globally equal; hence s=t.

Gluing: Given a compatible family s_i\in\mathfrak{M}(U_i) with s_i|_{U_i\cap U_j}=s_j|_{U_i\cap U_j} for all i,j, we construct a global section s\in\mathfrak{M}(U).

· Conscious component: Take the union of the proposition sets of the s_i, quotiented by logical equivalence on overlaps; compatibility guarantees no contradiction. A maximal consistent extension exists (by Zorn’s lemma).
· Subconscious component: Embeddings form continuous fields that glue on overlaps; the association graphs are united by identifying vertices on common sub‑graphs, which is well‑defined because of compatibility.
· Translation table: The union of the translation triples from each s_i is consistent on overlaps.

The constructed section restricts to the original s_i by construction, and the universal property of the equaliser holds because any family of maps equalised by the double product must factor uniquely through \mathfrak{M}(U). Thus \mathfrak{M} is a sheaf. ∎

Corollary 6.3. The meta‑cognitive layer possesses global coherence: local valid states can be uniquely integrated into a global understanding.

