7. Derived Galois Connection and Coherence

Define the composite functors

L = Q\circ F : \mathbf{CR} \to \mathbf{SP}, \qquad
R = G\circ P : \mathbf{SP} \to \mathbf{CR}.

Theorem 7.1 (Derived Galois Connection). For any C\in\mathbf{CR} and S\in\mathbf{SP},

L(C) \sqsubseteq S \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad C \sqsubseteq R(S).

Proof. Using the adjunctions F\dashv G and P\dashv Q, we obtain natural bijections

\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{SP}}(L C, S) \cong \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{Mii}}(F C, P S) \cong \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{CR}}(C, G P S) = \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{CR}}(C, R S).

When the hom‑sets are viewed as poset relations (existence of a morphism), this bijection exactly says L(C) \sqsubseteq S iff C \sqsubseteq R(S). ∎

Thus the cross‑translations form a Galois connection on the cpo of states. In particular, the fixed point (C^*,S^*) of Theorem 5.5 satisfies L(C^*) = S^* and R(S^*) = C^*, characterising it as a mutual fixed point of the two adjoint processes.

