8. Convergence of Learning Algorithms

We briefly state the convergence guarantees for the learning processes that update the numerical parameters (embeddings, weights, heuristic tables).

Theorem 8.1 (Stochastic Gradient Descent). Assume the loss function has Lipschitz continuous gradient, the gradient estimates are unbiased with bounded variance, and the step sizes \alpha_t satisfy the Robbins–Monro conditions \sum \alpha_t = \infty, \sum \alpha_t^2 < \infty. Then the iterates \theta_{t+1} = \theta_t - \alpha_t \nabla L_t(\theta_t) converge almost surely to a stationary point of the expected loss.

Theorem 8.2 (Evolutionary Tournament). An elitist genetic algorithm with positive mutation probability on a finite search space converges in probability to the global optimum of the fitness function.

Proof. These results are classical; see the stochastic approximation literature and the theory of evolutionary algorithms. ∎

