Appendix C. Functoriality of F (Formalisation)

Claim: F:\mathbf{CR}\to\mathbf{Mii} is a functor.

On objects: F(C) is defined explicitly in Section 3.1. The extraction of patterns from a trace is a deterministic function of the trace; since the trace is part of the object, F is well‑defined on objects.

On morphisms: Let f:C_1\to C_2. The morphism F(f) is defined as (F(C_1),F(C_2),\mathrm{Adj}_f,\mathrm{conf}(f)). We verify preservation of composition and identities.

Identity: If f = \mathrm{id}_C = (P,P,\text{empty},1.0), then F(f) has \mathrm{Adj} that adjusts nothing (since F(C) and F(C) are identical) and \mathrm{insight}=1.0. This is precisely \mathrm{id}_{F(C)}.

Composition: Let f:C_1\to C_2, g:C_2\to C_3. We have F(g\circ f) = (F(C_1),F(C_3),\mathrm{Adj}_{g\circ f},\mathrm{conf}(g\circ f)). Now

F(g)\circ F(f) = (F(C_1),F(C_3),\mathrm{Adj}_g \circ \mathrm{Adj}_f,\; \mathrm{conf}(f)\cdot\mathrm{conf}(g)\cdot\mathrm{compat}_{F}(f,g)),

where \mathrm{compat}_{F} is the compatibility factor defined in Mii. Because the balance adjustment for the composition is precisely the composite of the individual minimal adjustments, and the coherence \mathrm{coh}(f,g) used in CR is chosen to equal \mathrm{compat}_{F}(f,g), the two morphisms are identical. Hence F(g\circ f) = F(g)\circ F(f).

Therefore F is a functor. ∎

The functoriality proofs for G, P, Q, R follow analogous patterns and are omitted; they present no additional mathematical difficulty.

