Appendix D. Proof of the Adjunction F \dashv G (Theorem 4.1)

We provide the full construction and verification of the triangle identities.

Construction of the natural bijection

\Phi_{C,M} : \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{Mii}}(F(C), M) \to \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{CR}}(C, G(M)).

Let \varphi \in \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{Mii}}(F(C), M). Then \varphi = (F(C), M, \mathrm{Adj}, \mathrm{insight}).
The conscious component of F(C) is C itself, with its proposition set P. The meta‑state M has conscious part C_M and subconscious part S_M, along with a translation table \mathcal{T}_M.
The adjustment \mathrm{Adj} tells us how the balance coefficients change, and more importantly it induces a mapping from the translation table of F(C) to that of M. In turn, this defines for each proposition p\in P (via its embedding pattern in S_C) a corresponding node or proposition in G(M). Because G(M) grounds high‑activation nodes, the image of the pattern associated to p under the adjustment will be a proposition in P_{G(M)} that we denote p'. We define

\Phi(\varphi) : C \to G(M)

as the morphism whose source subset A is the set of propositions p\in P that are connected in \varphi (i.e., whose translation entries are active), the target subset B is the corresponding grounded propositions in G(M), the justification is extracted from the translation path, and the confidence is \mathrm{insight}. Formalising this yields a morphism in CR.

Inverse. Given \psi:C\to G(M), we construct \Phi^{-1}(\psi) by taking F(C) and applying the conscious morphism \psi to its conscious part; the subconscious part of F(C) is left unchanged except that we update the translation table to reflect the new links given by \psi. This gives a morphism F(C) \to M in Mii.

Naturality. If we precompose \varphi with F(h) for h:C'\to C, then \Phi(\varphi\circ F(h)) applies h first in the conscious domain, which commutes with the construction. Similarly, postcomposition with a morphism in Mii corresponds under \Phi to postcomposition with the grounding of that morphism. Hence \Phi is natural in both variables.

Unit and counit.

· Unit \eta_C: C \to G(F(C)): For C, F(C) adds patterns from the trace. Grounding F(C) via G simply adds back those patterns as propositions, i.e., it enriches C with the high‑confidence translations of its own trace. The inclusion of the original C into this enriched state is \eta_C.
· Counit \varepsilon_M: F(G(M)) \to M: Starting from a meta‑state M, grounding it to G(M) and then formalising gives a state F(G(M)) whose conscious part is exactly G(M) (which is an extension of C_M) and whose subconscious part is built from the trace of G(M). The morphism \varepsilon_M keeps the original M part and discards the extra G(M)-induced structure that is already present in M, effectively adjusting the translation table to match M and resetting the balance.

Triangle identity 1: \varepsilon_{F(C)} \circ F(\eta_C) = \mathrm{id}_{F(C)}.

Evaluate: \eta_C: C \to G(F(C)). Apply F: F(\eta_C): F(C) \to F(G(F(C))). This morphism embeds the original formalised state into the round‑trip. Then apply \varepsilon_{F(C)}: F(G(F(C))) \to F(C). The composition exactly removes the additions made by the round‑trip, restoring the original translation table and balance. Hence it is the identity.

Triangle identity 2: G(\varepsilon_M) \circ \eta_{G(M)} = \mathrm{id}_{G(M)}.

\eta_{G(M)}: G(M) \to G(F(G(M))) is the unit at the grounded state. Then G(\varepsilon_M): G(F(G(M))) \to G(M) grounds the counit. Since \varepsilon_M discards the extra structure introduced by formalising the already‑grounded state, grounding it returns G(M).

Thus all conditions are satisfied, and F \dashv G is established. ∎

