Appendix F. CR × SP as a CPO (Proposition 5.3)

We prove that the set of objects of \mathbf{CR}\times\mathbf{SP} with the relation \sqsubseteq is a complete partial order.

Partial order. Reflexivity: P \subseteq_{\mathrm{log}} P and V \subseteq V. Antisymmetry: If C_1 \sqsubseteq C_2 and C_2 \sqsubseteq C_1, then P_1 and P_2 have identical logical closures, hence are the same set of propositions up to equivalence, which we identify by taking the closure as part of the object. Likewise the vertex sets coincide, so the objects are equal. Transitivity follows from transitivity of inclusion and logical consequence.

Bottom element. (\emptyset,\emptyset) has the empty proposition set and empty association graph, and it is clearly a minimum.

Directed suprema. Let \{(C_i,S_i)\}_{i\in I} be a directed set. Define P^* = \bigcup_i P_i and V^* = \bigcup_i V_i. Because the set is directed, for any two propositions p\in P_i, q\in P_j there exists a k with P_i\subseteq P_k and P_j\subseteq P_k, so p,q coexist in P_k and any logical relation between them is consistent. The logical closure of P^* is therefore consistent. The association graph on V^* is defined by taking edge weights as the supremum over all i of the weights in S_i (when the edge exists; if it does not exist in some S_i, weight is 0). Because the family is directed, these weights stabilise on overlaps and yield a well‑defined graph. This defines the supremum (C^*,S^*) with P_{C^*} = \text{deductive closure}(P^*) and V_{\mathcal{A}^*} = V^*. The object belongs to CR×SP as the proposition set can be any consistent set, and the proof shows the supremum exists. Hence it is a cpo. ∎

