Appendix G. Scott‑continuity of T (Lemma 5.4)

We show T preserves directed suprema. Let \{(C_i,S_i)\}_{i\in I} be a directed subset with supremum (\bar{C},\bar{S}).

First, consider the component G(P(S_i)). The functor P lifts S_i to a meta‑state, extracting patterns as propositions. As S_i \sqsubseteq S_j implies more vertices, lifting more structures yields more propositions, and the conscious component of P(S_i) is monotone. Then G grounds the meta‑state, which again adds only propositions that are translations of high‑activation nodes. Since both P and G are monotone with respect to the underlying order, the chain G(P(S_i)) is directed in CR, and its supremum is the grounding of the supremum of the lifted states. Because P commutes with directed unions (pattern extraction from a union of association graphs is the union of the extractions) and G does (grounding a union of meta‑states is the union of their groundings), we have \sup_i G(P(S_i)) = G(P(\sup_i S_i)) = G(P(\bar{S})).

The same reasoning applies to Q(F(C_i)), yielding \sup_i Q(F(C_i)) = Q(F(\bar{C})).

Thus T(\sup_i (C_i,S_i)) = (G(P(\bar{S})), Q(F(\bar{C}))) = \sup_i (G(P(S_i)), Q(F(C_i))) = \sup_i T(C_i,S_i). Hence T is Scott‑continuous. ∎

