Appendix H. Proof of the Kleene Fixed‑Point Theorem (Theorem 1.7) – Classic

We recall the standard proof, included for completeness. Let f:D\to D be Scott‑continuous on a cpo D with bottom \bot. Define the Kleene chain by x_0 = \bot, x_{n+1} = f(x_n). Since \bot \sqsubseteq f(\bot) and f is monotone (which follows from Scott‑continuity), we have an increasing chain \bot \sqsubseteq f(\bot) \sqsubseteq f^2(\bot)\sqsubseteq\cdots. The set \{x_n\} is directed, so its supremum x^* = \bigsqcup_n x_n exists. By Scott‑continuity,

f(x^*) = f\bigl(\bigsqcup_n x_n\bigr) = \bigsqcup_n f(x_n) = \bigsqcup_n x_{n+1} = x^*.

Thus x^* is a fixed point. If y is any other fixed point, then \bot \sqsubseteq y and if x_n \sqsubseteq y then x_{n+1}=f(x_n)\sqsubseteq f(y)=y. By induction all x_n\sqsubseteq y, so x^* = \bigsqcup x_n \sqsubseteq y. Hence x^* is the least fixed point. ∎

The proof of Theorem 5.5 applies this theorem directly to T on the cpo defined in Appendix F, using the Scott‑continuity from Appendix G. This establishes the existence and minimality of (C^*,S^*).

