Appendix I. Sheaf Proof Details (Theorem 6.2)

We expand the verification of the sheaf condition for \mathfrak{M}.

Recall \mathfrak{M}(U) = \{ M_U \in \mathbf{Mii} \mid M_U \text{ is a valid meta‑state for } U \}. For a covering \{U_i\to U\} and U_{ij} = U_i\cap U_j, the sheaf diagram is

\mathfrak{M}(U) \xrightarrow{e} \prod_i \mathfrak{M}(U_i) \xrightarrow{p \atop q} \prod_{i,j} \mathfrak{M}(U_{ij}),

where e is restriction, and p,q are the two projections (restriction via U_{ij}\to U_i and U_{ij}\to U_j).

Equaliser: We must show e is an equaliser.

Injectivity (Locality): Suppose s,t\in \mathfrak{M}(U) have e(s)=e(t). Then for each i, s|_{U_i} = t|_{U_i}. The components of a meta‑state are the conscious tuple (P_U,\mathrm{Ctx}_U,\tau_U,\mathcal{W}_U), the subconscious tuple, the translation table, and balance coefficients. Restriction means we take the subset of propositions that are relevant to the sub‑problem, the sub‑graph of the association graph on the context, etc. If these restrictions agree for all U_i, and the U_i cover U (meaning the union of the contexts contains all elements of the context of U), then the global components must coincide. In particular, any proposition p\in P_U appears in some U_i (since the cover is jointly surjective on the space of sub‑problems; we define the category \mathcal{X} so that a sub‑problem includes the relevant propositions). Thus equality of the restricted sets forces equality of the full sets. Hence s=t.

Surjectivity onto the equaliser (Gluing): Let (s_i) \in \prod_i \mathfrak{M}(U_i) be such that p((s_i)) = q((s_i)), i.e. s_i|_{U_{ij}} = s_j|_{U_{ij}} for all i,j. We construct a global section s\in\mathfrak{M}(U) that restricts to each s_i.

· Conscious component: For each U_i, s_i includes a set of propositions P_i. On overlaps they are identical. Define P_U' = \bigcup_i P_i. If two propositions in the union are logically equivalent relative to the contexts of the overlap, we identify them. This yields a consistent set because any inconsistency would appear in some U_i, contradicting the validity of s_i. Let P_U be a maximal consistent extension (exists by Zorn's lemma; we can simply take the deductive closure of P_U' if the logic is compact). The context \mathrm{Ctx}_U is the union of the contexts of the U_i. The trace \tau_U is the concatenation (in any order, as traces are finite sequences) of the traces in s_i after filling in bridging steps if needed. The attention weights are extended by giving weight zero to new propositions.
· Subconscious component: For the embeddings, we have for each U_i a vector E_i\in\mathbb{R}^d. On overlap regions, the embeddings are required to agree (the restriction conditions include that the embeddings, when projected to the overlap's pattern space, are equal). Thus we can define a global embedding field by patching these local embeddings together; because the space of sub‑problems forms a manifold‑like poset, a partition of unity argument yields a global embedding that interpolates between them. More formally, we can take the direct sum of the vector spaces associated to each open and then take the quotient by equality on overlaps; the compatibility guarantees this is well‑defined. The same technique works for the association graph: we take the union of all vertices, identifying vertices that are mapped to each other by the transition maps on overlaps. Edge weights are defined as the maximum weight from any covering piece, which is consistent on overlaps.
· Translation table: Entries are triples (p,s,w). Compatibility means that if a triple appears in U_i and U_j, and the proposition p and node s are identified under the restriction mappings, then the weight w is the same. Hence we simply take the union of all triples; on overlaps the triples are identical, so no conflict.
· Balance coefficients: The coefficients \alpha,\beta,\gamma must be consistent. Since the balance is a meta‑parameter, it is global by definition. If local meta‑states have their own balances, compatibility forces them to be equal (otherwise the restriction could not hold). Thus we can pick the common value.

Now the constructed s restricts to each s_i: by construction, restricting the proposition set to the sub‑problem U_i discards propositions not originating from U_i, which are exactly the ones added from other pieces; the same for the association graph nodes and translation entries. Thus e(s) = (s_i).

Hence the diagram is an equaliser, and \mathfrak{M} is a sheaf. ∎

