Appendix J. Proof of the Derived Galois Connection (Theorem 7.1)

We prove the logical equivalence:

L(C) \sqsubseteq S \iff C \sqsubseteq R(S),

where L = Q\circ F and R = G\circ P.

Recall the partial order \sqsubseteq on CR is \subseteq_{\mathrm{log}} and on SP is inclusion of vertex sets (and corresponding embedding/subgraph). For the adjunction, we need to establish a relation between hom‑sets in the posetal sense.

From the adjunction F \dashv G, we have a natural bijection

\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{Mii}}(F(C), M) \cong \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{CR}}(C, G(M)).

When M = P(S), this gives

\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{Mii}}(F(C), P(S)) \cong \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{CR}}(C, G(P(S))) = \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{CR}}(C, R(S)).
\tag{1}

From the adjunction P \dashv Q, we have for any meta‑state M and subconscious S:

\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{Mii}}(M, P(S)) \cong \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{SP}}(Q(M), S).

Taking M = F(C),

\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{Mii}}(F(C), P(S)) \cong \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{SP}}(Q(F(C)), S) = \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{SP}}(L(C), S).
\tag{2}

Composing (1) and (2) we get

\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{SP}}(L(C), S) \cong \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{CR}}(C, R(S)).

Now, the hom‑set \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{SP}}(L(C), S) is a set of morphisms. However, in our category, a morphism L(C) \to S exists if and only if the vertex set of L(C) is a subset of that of S (up to the enriched similarity strength, but the existence of any morphism is what defines the order \sqsubseteq on objects, as we have identified the category with a preorder where hom-set non-emptiness equals order). Specifically, we define X \sqsubseteq Y in SP by the existence of a morphism X\to Y (e.g., an embedding). Under this definition, the category is equivalent to a poset. The same holds for CR, where C_1\sqsubseteq C_2 if there exists a morphism. With this identification, the bijection of hom‑sets is exactly the statement

L(C) \sqsubseteq S \iff C \sqsubseteq R(S).

Thus L and R form a (posetal) adjunction, i.e., a Galois connection. ∎

Corollary. At the fixed point (C^*,S^*) of T, we have T(C^*,S^*) = (C^*,S^*) meaning G(P(S^*)) = C^* and Q(F(C^*)) = S^*. Hence R(S^*) = C^* and L(C^*) = S^*; the Galois connection fixes both sides.


Theorem J.1. The functors L = Q\circ F : \mathbf{CR}\to\mathbf{SP} and R = G\circ P : \mathbf{SP}\to\mathbf{CR} form an adjunction L \dashv R.

Proof. Using the two adjunctions F\dashv G and P\dashv Q we obtain natural bijections

\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{SP}}(L C, S) \cong \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{Mii}}(F C, P S) \cong \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{CR}}(C, G P S) = \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{CR}}(C, R S).

Because the morphisms induce the order \sqsubseteq, this bijection is exactly L C \sqsubseteq S \iff C \sqsubseteq R S. Thus L\dashv R as a poset adjunction (Galois connection). The unit \eta^L : \mathrm{Id}_{\mathbf{CR}}\to R L and counit \varepsilon^L : L R \to \mathrm{Id}_{\mathbf{SP}} are derived from the units and counits of the two base adjunctions. ∎
