Appendix K. Convergence of Learning Algorithms (Theorems 8.1 and 8.2)

These are standard results. We provide a concise proof outline for completeness.

Theorem K.1 (SGD Convergence). Let \mathcal{L}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_z[\ell(\theta,z)] be a loss function such that \nabla\mathcal{L} is Lipschitz continuous with constant L. Let \nabla\ell(\theta,z) be an unbiased estimator of \nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta) with \mathbb{E}[\|\nabla\ell(\theta,z) - \nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta)\|^2] \le \sigma^2. Take step sizes a_t > 0 with \sum a_t = \infty and \sum a_t^2 < \infty. Then for the recursion \theta_{t+1} = \theta_t - a_t \nabla\ell(\theta_t, z_t), we have \nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta_t) \to 0 almost surely, and therefore \theta_t converges to a stationary point of \mathcal{L}.

Proof sketch. This follows from the classic Robbins–Monro theorem. The conditions ensure that the ordinary differential equation \dot{\theta} = -\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta) has the iterates as asymptotic pseudo‑trajectories, and Lyapunov stability of critical points yields convergence. See [5] for details. ∎

Theorem K.2 (Evolutionary Tournament). An elitist genetic algorithm with finite search space, positive mutation probability \mu > 0 applied to each individual per generation, and tournament selection converges in probability to the set of globally optimal solutions.

Proof sketch. The algorithm defines a Markov chain on the state (P_t) of population. Because the best fitness is never lost (elitism), and every point in the search space is reachable from any other via mutation with positive probability, the chain is absorbing at states containing a global optimum. Standard Markov chain arguments show that the probability of not having found an optimum after t generations decays exponentially, hence convergence in probability to the global optimum. ∎


The adjunction L \dashv R (Appendix J) gives rise to a monad on \mathbf{CR} and a comonad on \mathbf{SP}. We explicitly verify the monad laws for the monad R L on \mathbf{CR}; the comonad L R on \mathbf{SP} is entirely dual.

Definition K.1 (Monad R L).

· Functor: M = R L : \mathbf{CR}\to\mathbf{CR}, i.e. M(C) = G(P(Q(F(C)))).
· Unit: \eta^L : \mathrm{Id}_{\mathbf{CR}} \to R L from the adjunction.
· Multiplication: \mu = R \varepsilon^L L : R L R L \to R L, where \varepsilon^L : L R \to \mathrm{Id}_{\mathbf{SP}} is the counit of L\dashv R.

Theorem K.2. (M, \eta^L, \mu) satisfies the monad laws:

1. \mu \circ M\mu = \mu \circ \mu_M.
2. \mu \circ \eta^L_M = \mathrm{id}_M.
3. \mu \circ M\eta^L = \mathrm{id}_M.

Proof. These follow directly from the triangular identities of the adjunction L \dashv R. The verification is a standard exercise in category theory (see Mac Lane, Theorem VI.1.1). In the present concrete setting, the natural transformations calculate as expected:

\begin{aligned}
\mu_C : R L R L C \to R L C, \quad \mu_C = R( \varepsilon^L_{L C} ), \\
M\mu_C : R L R L R L C \to R L R L C, \quad M\mu_C = R L R( \varepsilon^L_{L C} ),
\end{aligned}

and the coherence conditions reduce to the equality of certain arrows in \mathbf{Mii} that hold because \varepsilon^L and \eta^L satisfy the adjunction equations. ∎

Corollary K.3. The fixed point (C^*,S^*) of Theorem 5.5 satisfies C^* \cong M(C^*) (i.e., it is a M-algebra) and dually S^* is a coalgebra for the comonad L R.

